    {
      "turn": 3,
      "doctor_question": "During these past fourteen months, have there been any periods of at least two weeks where your symptoms became significantly more intense, or where you experienced other changes like a complete loss of interest in all your usual activities, severe sleep problems, or thoughts that life isn't worth living?",
      "targeted_symptom": "S032",
      "patient_response": "Well, um, as far as sleep goes, yeah, I do have trouble. I mean, I'll be lying there in bed, staring at the ceiling fan—it has this little wobble to it, you know, just this rhythmic clicking sound that drives me crazy when the house is quiet—and I'll just be awake for hours before my early shift. The alarm is set for 5:30 AM, and I'll look at the clock at 2:00, then 3:15, and my mind is just racing through every single thing I've messed up. The mortgage, the tips I didn't make, the look on my wife's face when I told her I couldn't pick up an extra shift... it just plays on a loop. \n\nAnd, you know, you asked about thoughts of life not being worth living... I want to be clear, I don't have a plan to hurt myself. I really don't. I wouldn't do that to my family, especially with the debt we're in now. But... when I'm lying there, awake in the dark, listening to that ceiling fan, and I know I have to get up in a few hours and put on my apron and smile at customers while feeling like this absolute fraud... I do sometimes think it would just be so much easier if I just didn't wake up. Like, if I could just drift off and not have to face another day of failing at absolutely everything. It wouldn't be me giving up, exactly, it would just be... a relief. A break from the constant pressure and the guilt that I just can't seem to get out from under. So, yeah, those thoughts definitely creep in during those long nights, even though I'd never actually do anything to make it happen. It's just this heavy, exhausting wish for it all to stop.",
